<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Indian princely states</p><h1 class="st-title">Garhwal Kingdom</h1><p class="st-meta">888 CE – 1804 CE (approx.) · capital Srinagar (Garhwal)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Himalayan kingdom of the Panwar dynasty, which united the 52 forts of Garhwal and ruled the pilgrim country of the upper Ganges until the Gurkha conquest.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Tradition holds that Kanak Pal founded the Panwar dynasty at Chandpur Garhi in 888. For centuries the region was divided among many small chiefs, each holding a garh (fort). In the 14th or 15th century Raja <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ajay-pal">Ajay Pal</a> subdued them and united the country, which took its name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garhwal">Garhwal</a> from these forts.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ajay-pal">Ajay Pal</a> moved the capital to Devalgarh and then to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srinagar-uttarakhand">Srinagar</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alaknanda">Alaknanda</a>. The kingdom controlled the routes to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badrinath">Badrinath</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kedarnath">Kedarnath</a> and received pilgrims from across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Its Rajas, who used the title Shah from the 16th century, fought with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumaon">Kumaon</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fateh-shah-of-garhwal">Fateh Shah</a> opposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guru-gobind-singh">Guru Gobind Singh</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-bhangani">Bhangani</a> in 1688.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/gorkha-kingdom">Gorkha</a> army of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-nepal">Kingdom of Nepal</a> overran <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garhwal">Garhwal</a> and killed Raja <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pradyumna-shah">Pradyumna Shah</a> at Khurbura in 1804. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-nepalese-war">Anglo-Nepalese War</a> the British kept the eastern half and in 1815 restored his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudarshan-shah">Sudarshan Shah</a> to the western part, which became <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tehri-garhwal-state">Tehri Garhwal State</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/garhwal-kingdom.jpg" alt="Garhwal Kingdom" width="320" height="215"><figcaption>Image: Robert Alfers · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Princely_State_of_Tehri_Garhwal.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>888 CE – 1804 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Srinagar (Garhwal)</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ajay Pal, Fateh Shah, Pradyumna Shah</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Garhwali</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kingdom of Garhwal</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1346&amp;focus=garhwal-kingdom" aria-label="Garhwal Kingdom on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6716 1069 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Garhwal Kingdom lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7036 1325L7036 1325M7036 1325L7239 1418"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7036 1325h0M7239 1418h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7036 1325h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7036 1325h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1346&amp;focus=garhwal-kingdom">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
